# Rust Quality Guide (LLM Rulebook)

## Purpose

Testing and error handling rules for Rust projects. Apply when writing tests or handling errors. DO NOT apply to non-Rust code.

## Testing (MUST)

- Use `#[test]` for unit tests, `#[tokio::test]` for async tests
- Place unit tests in `#[cfg(test)]` modules within source files
- Place integration tests in `tests/` directory
- Use descriptive names: `test_subject_condition_result`

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    #[test]
    fn test_user_creation() {
        let user = User::new("test@example.com".to_string());
        assert_eq!(user.email, "test@example.com");
    }
}
```

## Error Handling (MUST)

- Define specific error types with `thiserror`
- Implement `std::error::Error` trait
- Use `?` operator for error propagation
- Avoid `Box<dyn std::error::Error>` in public APIs

```rust
#[derive(thiserror::Error, Debug)]
pub enum AppError {
    #[error("validation: {0}")]
    Validation(String),
    #[error("not found: {0}")]
    NotFound(String),
    #[error("database: {0}")]
    Database(#[from] sqlx::Error),
}
```

## Out of Scope

- Specific testing frameworks beyond built-in and tokio-test
- Custom CI/CD pipeline configurations
- Performance profiling tools beyond cargo-flamegraph
- Code coverage tools beyond cargo-tarpaulin